+// n nodes reachable by the in-order walk must SUM to exactly n, and no single node can be pointed at
+// more than three times (its parent and its two children; the root carries `m_root` instead of a
+// parent). A link held by a node the tree no longer owns is *not* part of the walk but still
+// increments its target, so `sum > n` by exactly the number of such stale links. That is the poison,
+// one step before it kills: the stale link keeps a count alive on a node that will then be deleted
